    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d646">

                <pb facs="0577" n="567" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg567"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00B/10–2749: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Strong</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-10-27T15:00:00+07:00">October 27, 1949—3
                            p. m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 10:54 p. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Cantel 1233. Reference telCan 734, October 26, 1 p.m.<anchor n="35"
                        xml:id="d646-f1"/> Best information here indicates Communist troops had not
                    crossed Kweichow border at any point. On contrary, Nationalists claim minor
                    successes in West Hunan by troops based Kweichow.</p>
                <p>As stated Cantel 1224, October 25,<anchor n="36" xml:id="d646-f2"/> it may be
                    expected Nationalists will make serious effort hold Kweichow and its
                    approaches.</p>
                <p>Reliable source also states Communists have not yet entered Kwangsi.</p>
                <p>Sent Department Cantel 1233. Department pass Kunming 34.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Strong</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d646-f1" n="35" xml:id="d646-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; it requested information regarding report of capture by Communist
                    General Lin Piao of Kweiyang (893.20/10–2649).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d646-f2" n="36" xml:id="d646-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
